(1) Translation:
Wei Wuhou planned a matter and succeeded, but none of the ministers could match him. After the court session, he showed a pleased expression. Wu Qi approached and said: “Have you ever heard the words of Chu Zhuangwang from your attendants?” Wuhou asked: “What were Chu Zhuangwang’s words?” Wu Qi replied: “Chu Zhuangwang planned a matter and succeeded, but none of the ministers could match him. After the court session, he showed a worried expression. Shen Gong Wuchen asked: ‘Your Majesty shows a worried expression after court—why?’ Zhuangwang said: ‘I planned a matter and succeeded, but none of the ministers could match me, so I am worried. In the words of the Zhongkui saying, it is said: “A lord who finds a teacher for himself becomes a king; who finds a friend becomes a hegemon; who finds a doubtful ally survives; who plans for himself and has no one like him perishes.” Now, with my unworthiness, and none of the ministers matching me, is my state about to perish? That is why I am worried.’ Chu Zhuangwang was worried, but your lordship is pleased.” Wuhou stammered, bowed twice, and said: “Heaven has sent your lordship to correct my fault.”

(2) Moral:
A ruler should not take pride in personal success when no one around him can equal him; instead, he should be humble and fearful, recognizing that his state depends on capable advisers and friends.

(3) Yes. The idea resembles Western notions of humility, self-examination, and the danger of pride—for example, in classical ethics and political thought, where rulers are warned against arrogance and encouraged to value counsel, wisdom, and humility. [end of text]


